Problem Statement



In a retail store, the inventory management system processes customer orders for products. Each customer orders a specific quantity of products.



The system must:

Process orders using multiple threads.
Ensure thread-safe access to the shared stock.
Preserve the input order when printing order results.


For each order:

If there is enough stock, the order is fulfilled and the inventory is updated.
If the stock is insufficient, the order is rejected, and the customer is notified.
Input format :
First line: An integer representing the initial stock of products.
Second line: An integer n, the number of customer orders.
Next n lines: Each line contains a string (customer name) and an integer (quantity), separated by a space.
Output format :
For each customer, print the result in the same order as input:

<customer name> successfully ordered <quantity> items.

<customer name> failed to order <quantity> items. Insufficient stock!



After all orders, print:

Final stock: <remaining stock>



Refer to the sample output for formatting specifications.

Code constraints :
0 ≤ initialStock ≤ 1000

0 ≤ numOrders ≤ 100

0 ≤ orderQuantity ≤ 1000

Customer names are non-empty strings containing only alphabets



Sample test cases :


      Input 1 :
          100
          3
          John 30
          Alice 50
          Bob 40
      Output 1 :
          John successfully ordered 30 items.
          Alice successfully ordered 50 items.
          Bob failed to order 40 items. Insufficient stock!
          Final stock: 20

            
      Input 2 :
          200
          4
          Michael 100
          Lisa 20
          David 50
          Sarah 80
      Output 2 :
          Michael successfully ordered 100 items.
          Lisa successfully ordered 20 items.
          David successfully ordered 50 items.
          Sarah failed to order 80 items. Insufficient stock!
          Final stock: 30
